use std
into sheet.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with sm_ is exported). It does two sheet-metal jobs. Planning: for the Sheet metal feature it works out the cross-section of every flange (a closed outline of straight pieces and arcs in a two-dimensional section plane), how far each flange sets back into the base, and the span along its base edge. Unfolding: for any bent sheet part it walks the smooth surface on one side of the sheet from its largest flat face across every bend, and maps every boundary edge of that surface into a flat pattern, unrolling each bend at its neutral radius. Angles given in or out are degrees; inside they are radians. A point (x, y) has a left normal (minus y, x). Rotating a two-dimensional vector (x, y) by an angle a gives (x·cos a minus y·sin a, x·sin a plus y·cos a). A bulge on a segment from S to E is the tangent of a quarter of the arc's signed sweep, positive when the arc turns counterclockwise.

```amni
extern fn sm_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "sm_view_f64";
extern fn sm_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "sm_view_i32";
```

Module state. Reals, all starting at 0.0: `NX0`, `NY0`, `NZ0` (a scratch vector) and `FX`, `FY` (a scratch flat point). Ints, all starting at 0: `NF`, `NE`, `NP` (faces, edges and points loaded for unfolding), `NG`, `NL` (base edges and flanges loaded for planning). Growable f64 arrays: `FC` (16 per face), `ED` (8 per edge), `PT` (3 per point), `PR` (12 unfolding parameters), `MP` (24 per face), `OS` (6 per output segment), `OB` (12 per output bend), `OI` (8 results), `GE` (8 per base edge), `FL` (20 per flange), `PP` (12 planning parameters), `OF` (24 per flange result), `OV` (3 per profile vertex), `SE` (1 per base edge). Growable i32 arrays: `EX` (1 per edge), `QU` (face queue), `FE` (1 per base edge).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` takes an i32 growable array by reference and does the same, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. `rs(xs, n)` and `ri(xs, n)` take an f64 or i32 growable array by reference: xs is emptied and then gets `reserve`(n). Every array in this file is sized only through these four helpers; no block defines other sizing helpers.

The exported function `sm_views()` registers every array JavaScript reads or writes: f64 views 1 `FC`, 2 `ED`, 3 `PT`, 4 `PR`, 5 `OS`, 6 `OB`, 7 `OI`, 8 `GE`, 9 `FL`, 10 `PP`, 11 `OF`, 12 `OV`, through sm_view_f64.

The exported function `sm_load(nf, ne, np)` takes three i32 counts and stores them in `NF`, `NE` and `NP`; with `zf` it sizes `FC` to 16·nf zeros, `ED` to 8·ne zeros, `PT` to 3·np zeros, `PR` to 12 zeros, `MP` to 24·nf zeros and `OI` to 8 zeros; `EX` gets `zi` of ne zeros, `QU` gets `ri` of nf, `OS` gets `rs` of 6 times 64 and `OB` gets `rs` of 12 times 16; then it calls `sm_views`.

The exported function `sm_load_plan(ng, nl)` takes two i32 counts and stores them in `NG` and `NL`; with `zf` it sizes `GE` to 8·ng zeros, `FL` to 20·nl zeros, `PP` to 12 zeros, `OF` to 24·nl zeros and `SE` to ng zeros; `FE` gets `zi` of ng entries equal to minus 1, `OV` gets `rs` of 3 times 64; then it calls `sm_views`.

A face record in `FC` (16 reals from 16·f): type (0 plane, 1 cylinder, 2 other), a point (for a plane a point on it, for a cylinder a point on its axis), a direction (for a plane its outward normal, for a cylinder its unit axis), the radius, the sign (for a cylinder plus 1 when its outward normal points away from the axis and minus 1 when it points toward it), the area, kin (the K-factor to use when this face is the inside of a bend), kout (the K-factor when it is the outside), a sample point on the face (3 reals) and a spare. An edge record in `ED` (8 reals from 8·e): its face, its partner edge (the same edge seen from the other face, or minus 1), its kind (0 a polyline through its points, 1 arcs through consecutive point triples), the index of its first point in `PT`, its point count and three spares.

`fnorm(f, x, y, z)` is the outward unit normal of face f at the point: for a plane its stored direction; for a cylinder the sign times the unit radial direction, where the radial direction is the point minus the axis point with its component along the axis removed. It leaves the normal in `NX0`, `NY0` and `NZ0`.

`radial(f, x, y, z)` puts into `NX0`, `NY0`, `NZ0` the unit radial direction of the point from the axis of face f (the point minus the axis point with its component along the axis removed, normalized).

A face map in `MP` (24 reals from 24·f) says where each point of the face goes in the flat. Slot 0 is 1 when the face is visited, slot 1 its kind (0 planar, 1 cylindrical) and slot 2 its depth (the number of bends from the start face). A planar map has an origin O (slots 3 to 5), two vectors E1 (slots 6 to 8) and E2 (slots 9 to 11) and a flat origin (slots 12, 13): a point X goes to the flat origin plus ((X minus O) dot E1, (X minus O) dot E2). A cylindrical map has a start point P (slots 3 to 5), the unit axis A (6 to 8), the unit radial direction U0 of P (9 to 11), the flat origin (12, 13), the flat axis direction DA (14, 15), the flat unroll direction DW (16, 17), the turn sign S (18), the neutral radius RN (19), the inside radius RI (20) and the K-factor (21). It sends X to the flat origin plus ax·DA plus phi·RN·DW, where ax is (X minus P) dot A, u is the unit radial direction of X, psi is the atan2 of ((U0 cross u) dot A) and (U0 dot u), phi is S·psi, and phi gets 2π added when it is below minus 0.001.

`flat(f, x, y, z)` maps a point with face f's map and leaves the result in `FX` and `FY`. `phiof(f, x, y, z)` returns phi for a cylindrical map and `axof(f, x, y, z)` returns ax.

`fromplane(g, f, x, y, z)` gives face g (a cylinder) a cylindrical map continuing face f's planar map across the shared edge point X: with A the axis direction of g, U0 the radial direction of X from g's axis, T the cross product A × U0, and C the sample point of g, S is 1 when (C minus X) dot T is above 0 and minus 1 otherwise, and W is S·T. P is X, DA is (A dot E1 of f, A dot E2 of f), DW is (W dot E1 of f, W dot E2 of f), the flat origin is the flat of X under f. RI is g's radius minus `PR`[0] when g's sign is above 0 and g's radius otherwise; the K-factor is `PR`[1] when that is at least 0, otherwise g's kout when g's sign is above 0 and g's kin otherwise; RN is RI plus K·`PR`[0]. Slot 1 becomes 1.

`fromcyl(g, f, x, y, z)` gives face g a planar map continuing face f's cylindrical map across the shared edge point X: with u the radial direction of X from f's axis and W equal to S of f times (A of f cross u), O is X, E1 is A·DA.x plus W·DW.x of f, E2 is A·DA.y plus W·DW.y of f (each a 3-vector), and the flat origin is the flat of X under f. Slot 1 becomes 0.

`copymap(g, f)` copies slots 1 and 3 to 23 of face f's map to face g.

`transfer(g, f, e)` builds face g's map from face f's across edge e, using X equal to the first point of e: when f is planar and g is a plane it calls `copymap`; when f is planar and g a cylinder it calls `fromplane`; when f is cylindrical and g a plane it calls `fromcyl`; when f is cylindrical and g a cylinder whose axis is parallel to f's (absolute dot of the axes above 0.9999) and whose radius matches f's within 1e-6 of the larger radius plus 1e-9, it calls `copymap`; otherwise (two different bends meeting) it first calls `fromcyl` on g (giving g a planar map at X) and then `fromplane` with g as both the target and the source. Slot 0 of g becomes 1 and its depth is f's depth plus 1 when f is planar and g a cylinder, otherwise f's depth.

`pickstart()` returns the start face: `PR`[2] as an int when it is at least 0, below `NF` and that face is a plane; otherwise among the planes it finds the largest area, and among planes whose area is at least 0.999 times that largest it returns the one with the largest normal z, then the largest normal y, then the largest normal x; minus 1 when there is no plane.

`startmap(s)` gives face s a planar map: n is its normal, x is (1, 0, 0) unless the absolute normal x is above 0.9, then (0, 1, 0); E1 is x minus (x dot n)·n, normalized; E2 is n cross E1; O is the face point; the flat origin is (O dot E1, O dot E2). Slots 0 and 1 become 1 and 0 and the depth 0.

`smooth(e)` is true when edge e has a partner and the outward normals of both faces at the edge's middle point (its point at index first plus count div 2) have a dot product of at least `PR`[3] (0.9999 when `PR`[3] is not above 0), and both faces are planes or cylinders.

`walk()` does a breadth-first walk with `QU` used as a queue with a read index, starting from the start face already pushed. For each face f taken it looks at every edge e whose face is f and whose partner p is at least 0, with g the face of p. When `smooth`(e) holds, `EX`[e] and `EX`[p] become 1 and, when g is not visited, it calls `transfer`(g, f, e) and pushes g. When the partner's face is a smooth neighbor of type 2 (other), `OI`[2] becomes 2.

`emit()` writes the flat boundary into `OS`: for every edge e whose face is visited and whose `EX` is 0, kind 0 maps its points in order and appends each consecutive pair as a segment (e, x0, y0, x1, y1, 0) when the pair is at least 1e-9 apart; kind 1 maps the points and, for each triple S, M, E starting at the first point and stepping by 2, appends (e, S, E, b) with b equal to minus 2 times the cross product of (E minus S) and (M minus S) divided by the squared length of E minus S, skipping triples whose ends are within 1e-9. While emitting it tracks the smallest and largest flat x and y into `OI`[4] to `OI`[7].

`bends()` appends one bend to `OB` for each visited face whose map is cylindrical: over every point of every edge of the face it finds the smallest and largest phi (p0, p1) and the smallest and largest ax (a0, a1); the center line runs from the flat of axial a0 to axial a1 at phi equal to the middle of p0 and p1 (the flat origin plus ax·DA plus that phi·RN·DW). The record is: face, x0, y0, x1, y1, the sweep p1 minus p0 in degrees, the direction (minus 1 when the face's sign is above 0, else 1), RI, the bend allowance (p1 minus p0)·RN, the K-factor, the length a1 minus a0 and the depth.

The exported function `sm_unfold()` returns an i32. It sets `OI` entries 4 and 5 to 1e30 and 6 and 7 to minus 1e30, `OS` and `OB` emptied keeping their reserve, sizes `MP` with `zf` to 24·`NF` zeros, `EX` with `zi` to `NE` zeros and empties `QU`. s is `pickstart`(); when s is minus 1 it sets `OI`[2] to 1, calls `sm_views` and returns 1. Otherwise `OI`[3] is s, it calls `startmap`(s), pushes s on `QU` and calls `walk`, `emit` and `bends`. `OI`[0] is the number of visited faces and `OI`[1] their total area. It calls `sm_views` and returns `OI`[2] as an i32.

A flange record in `FL` (20 reals from 20·i): its base edge index, up (1 or minus 1), its segment count n (1 to 7), its bend radius (0 means use `PP`[1]), then n pairs of a length and an angle in degrees starting at slot 4. `PP` holds the thickness t, the default bend radius, the corner mode (1 butt, 0 open) and the corner gap. A base edge record in `GE` (8 reals from 8·g): x0, y0, x1, y1 of a straight edge of the base outline in counterclockwise order, 1 when it shares its start point with the previous edge, 1 when it shares its end point with the next edge, and two spares.

`setback(r, t, a, left)` is the setback of a bend of angle a (radians): r plus t when a is at least 179 degrees; otherwise (r plus t) times the tangent of half of a when left is true and r times that tangent when it is false. Segment j (counting from 1) turns left when j is 1 or its angle is above 0.

`sm_plan()` returns an i32: 0 when every flange planned, otherwise the largest status. It empties `OV` keeping its reserve. A first pass sets, for each flange i with a valid edge index g (0 up to `NG`), `FE`[g] to i when `FE`[g] is minus 1 and otherwise marks status 5 (two flanges on one edge) in `OF`[24i]; and `SE`[g] to the setback of its first segment. A second pass calls `plan1`(i) for each flange; then it calls `sm_views`, and the result is the largest `OF`[24i].

`plan1(i)` plans flange i into `OF` (24 reals from 24i: status, setback, e0, e1, x0, x1, the index of its first vertex in `OV` divided by 3, its vertex count, the four corners of its first flat as eight reals p, q at slots 8 to 15, the edge length at 16, the failing segment at 17 and its minimum length at 18). It keeps a status 5 set by the first pass and stops there. An edge index outside 0 up to `NG` gives status 2. With t from `PP`[0], r the flange's radius or `PP`[1], n its segments, each angle's absolute value in radians and each segment's setback s from `setback`: an angle of 0 or above 180 degrees gives status 3; s for segment n plus 1 is 0; each flat length F of segment j is its length minus its setback minus the next setback, and when F is below minus 1e-9 the status is 1 with the segment j at slot 17 and its setback plus the next one at slot 18, and F is used as at least 0.

The profile is a reference line R and an offset line O. R starts at A equal to (minus the first setback, minus t) heading d equal to (1, 0). For segment j with sigma 1 when it turns left and minus 1 otherwise, rho is r plus t when it turns left and r otherwise: the center C is A plus sigma·rho times the left normal of d, the new heading d2 is d rotated by sigma times the angle, B is C minus sigma·rho times the left normal of d2; when R is empty it first gets A with heading d; the last point of R (which is A) then gets bulge sigma times the tangent of a quarter of the angle, so A is never added twice; then R gets B with heading d2 (bulge 0); when F is above 1e-12 the end E is B plus F·d2 and R gets E with heading d2 (bulge 0); then A is the last point of R and d is d2. Every point of R gets an offset point: the point plus t times the left normal of its heading. The output vertices are R in order with their bulges (the last one 0), then the offset points in reverse order, where the offset of R point k carries bulge minus the bulge of R point k minus 1 (and the offset of the first point 0); each vertex is p, q, bulge appended to `OV`. The first flat's corners are B of segment 1, the following R point, and the offsets of that point and of B (when F of segment 1 is not above 1e-12 all four are B and its offset).

The span: with the edge from (x0, y0) to (x1, y1), L its length, u its unit direction and nin the left normal of u, and s1 the first setback. At the start, when the edge shares its start with the previous edge (index g minus 1, or `NG` minus 1 for g equal to 0), up is that edge's unit direction, np its left normal, sn its `SE`, and when the absolute value of u dot np is above 1e-9, e0 is the larger of 0 and (sn minus s1·(nin dot np)) over (u dot np); otherwise e0 is 0. At the end, when the edge shares its end with the next edge (g plus 1, or 0 after the last), with that edge's direction, left normal nn and `SE` sn, and the absolute value of u dot nn above 1e-9, the end gap is the larger of 0 and (s1·(nin dot nn) minus sn) over (u dot nn), else 0, and e1 is L minus the end gap. When e1 minus e0 is at most 1e-9 the status is 4. x0 and x1 are 0 unless `PP`[2] is 1: at the start, when the previous edge is linked, has a flange (its `FE` at least 0) that has the same up, the absolute value of u dot that edge's direction is below 1e-6, and the first angles of both flanges are within 0.01 of 90 degrees, x0 is e0 when this edge is longer than the previous one or as long with a smaller index (it overlaps), and the larger of 0 and e0 minus t minus `PP`[3] otherwise; the end is the same with the next edge, L minus e1 and x1. The record gets the status (0 when nothing failed), s1, e0, e1, x0, x1, the vertex start, the vertex count, the first flat corners and L.
